WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.160
Even the most seemingly invincible bad guys&nbsp;
usually succumb to the hero by the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:12.000
movie, but the rare occasions they don't can&nbsp;
make for a memorable experience. Be warned:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.880
We're digging into spoilers galore in the&nbsp;
best movies where villains win in the end.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.240
A story with roots in history, The&nbsp;
Crucible takes place way back at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:28.480
end of the 17th century in colonial America in&nbsp;
the sleepy town of Salem, Massachusetts. Yep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.040
that Salem. Inspired by the Salem&nbsp;
Witch Trials of historic legend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.200
the film follows a group of young&nbsp;
women, including Abigail Williams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.200
played by Winona Ryder, who has had an affair&nbsp;
with Daniel Day-Lewis's John Proctor and literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:49.040
wants to put a curse on his wife in order to&nbsp;
kill her off and have Proctor all to herself.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.920
Already messed up in the special kind of way&nbsp;
that only real-life scenarios can provide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:59.520
the situation begins to spin out of control when&nbsp;
Abigail and the group of girls are nearly caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.320
practicing witchcraft and ultimately begin&nbsp;
to accuse others to save their own skin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:10.720
Of course, taking place in Puritan New England,&nbsp;
these kinds of things were not taken lightly, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:16.720
anyone who is accused by the supposedly innocent&nbsp;
posse of girls has to either confess or be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.480
executed. The film ends with several characters,&nbsp;
including John Proctor himself, refusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:28.040
to confess and ultimately being killed. Talk&nbsp;
about a depressing win for the forces of evil.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.640
Unlike a lot of horror flicks, the triumph&nbsp;
of Saw's villain comes as a clever surprise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.000
Whether you're a fan of the franchise or not, it's&nbsp;
hard not to be impressed by the twist ending that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.040
the first film in the series delivered, with&nbsp;
Jigsaw rising up from the center of the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.800
bringing everyone's jaws crashing to the ground.&nbsp;
While director James Wan's debut film was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:56.640
horrifying success, that doesn't change the fact&nbsp;
that the villain yet again wins without question.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.800
One of the first revelations of the film&nbsp;
is that the saws so helpfully provided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.160
the captive protagonists are&nbsp;
meant for cutting not metal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:11.040
but flesh. The climax becomes increasingly tense&nbsp;
as Dr. Lawrence Gordon, played by Cary Elwes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.000
slowly loses his mental stability. But even&nbsp;
before the saws come into play, the string of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.960
events orchestrated by the mysterious murderer&nbsp;
are more than enough to showcase that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:26.640
is a villain who knows how to win. From bear&nbsp;
traps to barbed wire and more, Jigsaw is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.280
of the most successful villains to ever lay&nbsp;
in a pool of fake blood on a bathroom floor.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.240
The early 1970s were a dark&nbsp;
time for American movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:42.400
and Chinatown is exemplary of that trend. The&nbsp;
film is stylized like a noir, complete with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:48.000
1930s setting and a complicated plot involving&nbsp;
water rights in California. The movie's plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.240
is ultimately fairly secondary to its tone, which&nbsp;
is despairing throughout. In its final moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:00.160
we see the death of the film's heroine played by&nbsp;
Faye Dunaway, and hear the film's signature line:

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.840
"Forget it Jake, it's Chinatown."

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.920
The film's villain, Noah Cross, played by John&nbsp;
Huston, gets away with his complicated scheme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.880
to consolidate more power. What's more, no&nbsp;
one discovers that his daughter, who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:22.160
the one who initially opened the case with Jack&nbsp;
Nicholson's private investigator character Jake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.680
Gittes , was someone he had abused practically&nbsp;
all her life. At the film's conclusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.960
Gittes fails to protect her and is forced to&nbsp;
walk away from the whole affair unsatisfied.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.080
Chinatown is a grim film about the realities&nbsp;
of life -- where a few powerful people can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:43.840
play with the rest of us like pieces on a chess&nbsp;
board. Gittes tries his best to change things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.360
but discovers that knowing the truth&nbsp;
matters less than having power.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.600
The Coen brothers don't usually make happy&nbsp;
movies, but No Country for Old Men is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.880
of the filmmaking duo's bleakest. It&nbsp;
follows Josh Brolin's Llewelyn Moss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.360
as he makes off with a huge pile of cash&nbsp;
in the aftermath of a drug deal gone wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:10.720
He's pursued by Javier Bardem's Anton Chigurh,&nbsp;
a man relentlessly bent on recovering the funds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.880
Chigurh is a menacing figure -- who can&nbsp;
forget the infamous coin flip scene?"

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:18.500
"Call it."

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:19.760
"Call it?"

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.000
"Yes."
"For what?"

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:22.640
"Just call it."

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.280
Both Moss and Chigurh are also being&nbsp;
pursued by Tommy Lee Jones' character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.000
Sheriff Tom Bell, who makes a vow to&nbsp;
protect Moss but ultimately fails.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.440
Chigurh kills him offscreen, recovers the&nbsp;
money, and walks away scot free. No Country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:45.120
for Old Men ends with Bell, who has retired from&nbsp;
the force as a result of the violence he's seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.400
as he describes a dream he once had about&nbsp;
his father and finding light in the darkness.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:56.320
That ending, and the entirety of No Country for&nbsp;
Old Men, suggests that light is an illusion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:02.000
Humanity lives in the darkness and revels in it,&nbsp;
and bad people do bad things and get away with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.760
them. Anton Chigurh isn't punished for his crimes.&nbsp;
He's brutal enough to do whatever he wants.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:14.080
Rosemary's Baby follows Mia Farrow as Rosemary,&nbsp;
as she goes through her pregnancy and the birth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.120
of her child, only to discover that she's&nbsp;
delivered a child of Satan. The baby was part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.440
of a deal that her husband drew up with their&nbsp;
neighbors, who happen to worship the devil.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.160
Throughout the film, Rosemary works to uncover&nbsp;
what's really going on with her pregnancy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:33.840
but in the end, she fails to stop the demonic&nbsp;
scheme. What's more, she seems to decide that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.280
she's willing to love the child anyway. She was&nbsp;
forced into this pregnancy against her will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.360
sold out by her husband&nbsp;
completely. Her child, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.280
is still hers, even if his dad&nbsp;
is a bit of a troublesome figure.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.240
The movie ends on the bleak idea that Rosemary&nbsp;
can't do anything about her situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.840
She's been tortured throughout the film, and her&nbsp;
reward is a child she never asked for. Evil wins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.640
out, and Rosemary is left to pick up the pieces&nbsp;
of a life that other people have forced on her.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:08.800
If your wife tried to frame you for her killing&nbsp;
her, would you take her back when she returned?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:14.080
Most people would likely say no, but not Ben&nbsp;
Affleck's Nick Dunn, one of the characters at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.280
the center of Gone Girl. The movie follows Nick&nbsp;
in the aftermath of his wife's disappearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:24.560
as evidence begins to pile up suggesting he's&nbsp;
the killer. As we learn midway through the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:30.240
though, his wife Amy, played by Rosamund&nbsp;
Pike, has been setting him up the entire time.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.600
From there, the movie tracks&nbsp;
Amy's decision to return home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.200
and her ultimate plan to trap&nbsp;
Nick in their relationship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.840
She's pregnant with his child, and he&nbsp;
decides that he has to stay to help raise it.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:47.360
As Amy, the film's ostensible villain, tells&nbsp;
him, he actually likes the life she gives him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:50.480
She's exciting and challenging,&nbsp;
and that's what he wants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:55.520
even if it means that he's occasionally&nbsp;
accused of murder. In the end of Gone Girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.640
Amy gets everything that she wants, even though&nbsp;
she's killed at least one person along the way.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:06.960
Few movies pull the rug out from audiences&nbsp;
as successfully as The Usual Suspects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.480
For most of its runtime, we think&nbsp;
we're getting a reliable version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.600
the film's events from Kevin Spacey's Verbal Kint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:19.280
the only survivor of a five-man crew who decides&nbsp;
to pull a robbery that goes terribly awry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.320
In the film's final moments, though, we realize&nbsp;
that Verbal has been lying the entire time.

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:28.400
The film's villain, a terrible&nbsp;
criminal known only as Keyser Soze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:34.240
is actually Kint, and the limp he's had for&nbsp;
the entire movie is fake. It's unclear how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:39.520
truth there is in the story that Kint tells,&nbsp;
but he ultimately escapes without a scratch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.680
He deceives everyone, and the movie's&nbsp;
final moments are devoted to watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.080
Kint drive away, having killed several&nbsp;
people without any sort of punishment.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:53.040
Spacey's performance in the film won&nbsp;
him an Oscar, and it's easy to see why.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.360
There's a menace hiding underneath the&nbsp;
supposedly vulnerable Kint that becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.560
obvious as soon as you know the twist. Kint is&nbsp;
just telling a story to get out of a tough bind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:06.160
and the cops who are listening&nbsp;
fall for it hook, line, and sinker.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:12.640
Erotic thrillers thrive on the bizarre&nbsp;
combination of sensual tension and death threats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:18.240
In Basic Instinct, Sharon Stone and Michael&nbsp;
Douglas balance those qualities perfectly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:23.920
The film follows Douglas' erratic cop as&nbsp;
he becomes obsessed with Stone's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:27.360
an author who is a suspect in a&nbsp;
homicide case in part because she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.360
wrote a book that describes a very&nbsp;
similar killing in gruesome detail.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:37.040
Douglas' obsession eventually transforms into a&nbsp;
romantic relationship, and audiences are never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:42.560
explicitly told that Stone's character is guilty.&nbsp;
Even so, it's easy to put the pieces together and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:47.520
realize that she's likely gotten away with several&nbsp;
killings over the course of the film — possibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:51.840
before as well. All of that becomes more&nbsp;
evident in the film's closing moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:55.680
as we see that Stone's author has&nbsp;
considered killing Douglas' cop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.960
using the same method as in the&nbsp;
death at the start of the film.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:03.200
Basic Instinct is a filthy neo-noir&nbsp;
in every sense of the word.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.400
While we don't know for sure&nbsp;
who the murderer really is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:11.440
it certainly seems like someone has gotten&nbsp;
off easy as the movie reaches its conclusion.

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:17.040
The Alien movies are generally fairly&nbsp;
bleak affairs. In the first couple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:21.920
though, protagonist Ellen Ripley gets out of&nbsp;
danger by the end, even if there are heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:27.360
casualties along the way. By the time Ridley&nbsp;
Scott got around to directing Alien: Covenant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:32.480
that had changed. Unlike most previous Alien&nbsp;
movies, though, the villain of Covenant isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:37.840
a xenomorph, but an android named David, played&nbsp;
by Michael Fassbender, who was first introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:43.520
in the preceding film, Prometheus. In this movie,&nbsp;
we learn that David is the xenomorph's creator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:47.680
and we watch as he slowly picks off the&nbsp;
crew of the ship that has discovered him.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:53.520
Although several crew members do survive the&nbsp;
film, David undoubtedly emerges victorious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:58.480
and successfully escapes the planet where he was&nbsp;
stranded. He's going to spread xenomorphs all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:04.160
the galaxy, and one of those very same aliens&nbsp;
will eventually terrorize Ripley and her crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:08.320
on the Nostromo. David's victory in this&nbsp;
film is not just bleak because of what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:13.440
means about Covenant, but also because of what it&nbsp;
foretells about the rest of the Alien universe.&nbsp;

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.840
One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest&nbsp;
is about a rebel who loses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:23.360
The film stars Jack Nicholson as Randall&nbsp;
McMurphy, a new patient at a mental institution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:28.240
who pleaded insanity in order to avoid hard&nbsp;
labor while imprisoned. Once he arrives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:33.120
he discovers that the hospital is being run&nbsp;
under the strict supervision of Nurse Ratched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:37.360
and decides that he wants to challenge&nbsp;
her rule. The film chronicles McMurphy's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:45.840
attempts to rile the other patients at the&nbsp;
institute up in defiance of Nurse Ratched.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.800
"It's a long fly ball to deep left center&nbsp;
- it's going, it's going, it's gone!"

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:55.760
Ultimately, though, McMurphy's attempts at&nbsp;
defiance fail. At the end of One Flew Over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:01.040
the Cuckoo's Nest, it's determined that the only&nbsp;
way the threat McMurphy poses can be neutralized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:06.400
is if he is lobotomized. In the film's final&nbsp;
scene, Chief, a character who is believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:11.920
to be non-verbal throughout most of the film -&nbsp;
only to be revealed to be basically faking it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:16.560
asphyxiates McMurphy with a pillow and&nbsp;
escapes from the institute. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:21.280
Chief gets away with his life, McMurphy,&nbsp;
the rebel at the film's center, does not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:26.240
Nurse Ratched quashes his attempt to get&nbsp;
himself and his fellow patients more freedom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:30.480
and he dies. Ratched's tyrannical&nbsp;
rule over the facility remains.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:35.360
The Cabin in the Woods is something&nbsp;
of an exception on this list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:40.320
The villains undoubtedly win, but they do so&nbsp;
with the help of the movie's protagonists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:45.360
The Cabin in the Woods is an explicit riff&nbsp;
on horror movie tropes, as five friends head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:50.240
to the country to spend a weekend in a creepy,&nbsp;
abandoned cabin. What they don't know is that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:56.800
are actually human sacrifices in an annual ritual.&nbsp;
The ritual has specific rules that happen to align&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:02.080
with those of a horror movie, and requires that at&nbsp;
least four of the five friends die in the cabin.

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:06.800
If the friends don't die, mythical forces from&nbsp;
the underworld will return to Earth and wipe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:12.320
out mankind. When the main characters discover&nbsp;
this, though, they decide not to play along.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:16.240
Instead of agreeing to act as&nbsp;
sacrifices, they decide that humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:21.440
should be doomed. The ritual fails, and&nbsp;
the titans return to Earth. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:26.480
the film's human villains, the people who were&nbsp;
trying to sacrifice our main characters, lose.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:33.200
Watchmen is based on some pretty depressing&nbsp;
source material, and Zack Snyder's 2009&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:38.160
adaptation doesn't do much to mess with that&nbsp;
tone. Although the adaptation does change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:42.080
some things, the basic plot of&nbsp;
the graphic novel remains intact:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:45.920
The death of a superhero spurns&nbsp;
an investigation that ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:51.360
unveils a plot hatched by Matthew Goode's&nbsp;
Ozymandias, the smartest man on the planet.

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:57.760
Ozymandias' plan is to avert an apocalyptic event&nbsp;
by creating one on a much smaller scale. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:03.040
movie, this event is a radioactive explosion&nbsp;
that he plans to blame on Dr. Manhattan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:08.400
uniting the U.S. and Soviet Union against him.&nbsp;
Ultimately, the rest of the superheroes agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:13.520
that Ozymandias' plan is the best course of&nbsp;
action if an apocalypse is to be avoided.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:17.920
The plan requires the deaths of millions,&nbsp;
but it goes off without a hitch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:22.240
and Ozymandias believes that he'll be&nbsp;
justified in the end. Both the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:27.200
and the graphic novel suggest that the plan may&nbsp;
not be the success that he imagines it to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:32.720
but they allow him to carry it out anyway. He&nbsp;
wins, and his victory means death and destruction.

00:13:33.756 --> 00:13:38.720
The original 1973 version of The Wicker&nbsp;
Man ends with a human sacrifice, as all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:44.000
great horror movies should. The film follows&nbsp;
a devout Christian detective named Neil Howie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:48.560
who visits a small Hebridean island as&nbsp;
part of his search for a missing girl.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:53.760
While on the island, Howie becomes horrified&nbsp;
by the pagan rituals that he sees practiced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:58.080
Ultimately, he determines that the missing&nbsp;
girl is meant to be a human sacrifice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:01.920
and infiltrates the islanders'&nbsp;
ceremony in order to rescue her.

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:04.880
After escaping with her, though,&nbsp;
it's revealed that she was just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:10.240
ruse to draw Howie to the island. Howie was&nbsp;
always intended to be the human sacrifice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:14.800
as the sacrifice has to meet certain&nbsp;
criteria, and he meets all of them. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:20.480
movie ends with our hero detective being burned&nbsp;
alive alongside a variety of animal sacrifices.

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:26.960
Playing on the paranoia caused by terrorist&nbsp;
bombers in the 1990s, Arlington Road is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:31.440
suspecting that your neighbors are terrorists...&nbsp;
and ultimately finding out that you're right. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:37.200
movie follows Michael Faraday as he meets his new&nbsp;
neighbors and begins to suspect that they're lying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:42.080
about their lives. Ultimately, he discovers&nbsp;
that they're part of a terrorist organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:46.880
and their goal is to blow up part of an FBI&nbsp;
building located near their neighborhood.

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:52.000
Although Michael attempts to warn the authorities&nbsp;
about the incoming attack, his neighbors are ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:57.440
of him at every turn. Ultimately, a bomb does&nbsp;
go off, but the bomb has been planted in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:02.480
back of Michael's car. Because Michael was at&nbsp;
the FBI building to warn them, it blows part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:07.680
of that building up, and Michael appears to be&nbsp;
the lone wolf responsible for the explosion.

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:12.000
Michael's neighbors, meanwhile, get away&nbsp;
scot free, and move to another suburb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.720
where they can find someone else&nbsp;
to blame for their latest attack.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:20.720
There aren't really any good guys in Ex Machina,&nbsp;
but that doesn't mean that the villain doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:25.760
ultimately win the day. The movie, which tells&nbsp;
the story of Domhnall Gleeson's low-level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:30.880
computer programmer named Caleb who travels&nbsp;
to a tech billionaire's house in order to test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:38.720
his advanced artificial intelligence, exists in a&nbsp;
moral gray area. Ava, the artificial intelligence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:44.240
seems kind and gentle, but obviously resents&nbsp;
the cage she has been placed in by her creator.

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:48.720
Eventually, Ava escapes that cage,&nbsp;
killing her billionaire creator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:53.760
and trapping Caleb inside her former home. The&nbsp;
movie treats her escape with a sense of dread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:58.560
and although she sends some characters to their&nbsp;
doom, it's hard not to feel at least part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:04.320
yourself rooting for Ava. She's been trapped&nbsp;
by mankind for the entirety of Ex Machina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:07.840
and in the end, she finally&nbsp;
gets a chance to break free.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:12.480
If you're just looking at its premise,&nbsp;
Inside Man really shouldn't work. The movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:16.640
which tells the story of bank robbers&nbsp;
and the cops who are trying to stop them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:21.200
spends much of its running time hyping up&nbsp;
the genius of the robbery plot at its center.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:26.160
At times, it seems like the head robber, played&nbsp;
by Clive Owen, is losing control of his carefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:31.760
constructed plan. And because the cop in charge&nbsp;
of stopping him is played by Denzel Washington,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:35.440
it can be pretty hard to imagine&nbsp;
the robbery going successfully.

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:38.640
In the end, though, our robber gets&nbsp;
away with it. He hides inside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:43.040
bank for days after the initial robbery,&nbsp;
waiting for the right moment to casually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:47.600
exit the building with all the money he's&nbsp;
stolen. It's a shockingly simple plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:53.120
one that only requires some determination on his&nbsp;
part. Washington's cop steps away from the crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:57.920
realizing there's an entire rigged system that&nbsp;
he's operating within. At the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:02.720
Owen's character gets away with it because the&nbsp;
cop charged with stopping him stopped caring.

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:06.640
Mr. Glass, the villain at&nbsp;
the heart of Unbreakable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:10.720
believes in the archetypes that comic&nbsp;
books establish. He knows he's a villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:16.000
and that David Dunn exists to oppose him. The&nbsp;
movie's twist isn't that Glass has pulled off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:21.200
some major scheme. What we discover is that&nbsp;
he's been a terrorist this entire time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:25.920
and that his ultimate goal was to uncover&nbsp;
other people with special abilities like his.

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:30.640
Glass gets away with his plot because he carries&nbsp;
it out before the movie even really begins. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:36.240
discovers David in the process, and while David&nbsp;
ultimately imprisons him, he's actually doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:41.680
exactly what Glass wants him to. Glass's&nbsp;
only goal was to find a hero to oppose him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:46.320
and he's done that by discovering David. He&nbsp;
never wanted to escape from his plan without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:51.280
consequences. He wants to fulfill his comic&nbsp;
book fantasy, with everything that might entail.

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:57.200
Matt Damon has a range he doesn't get to show&nbsp;
off much these days. In The Talented Mr. Ripley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:02.560
though, we see Damon play a psychopath with a&nbsp;
chilling skill. The movie follows Damon's Tom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:08.320
Ripley, a low-class man who rises up the&nbsp;
ranks of young high society in the 1950s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:14.000
thanks to a relationship with Jude Law's&nbsp;
Dickie Greenleaf, a wealthy, aimless young man.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:20.080
Ultimately, Tom's love for and jealousy of Dickie&nbsp;
proves too powerful, and he kills him at sea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:25.760
Instead of confessing to his crime, though, Tom&nbsp;
takes over Dickie's life, and all of the wealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:31.040
that it entails. Tom spends the rest of the&nbsp;
film trying desperately not to get caught, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:37.280
he succeeds. He gets away with that murder — and&nbsp;
another one. Tom Ripley may not be a good person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:42.240
but he is very good at what he does, and he&nbsp;
proves that at the end of The Talented Mr. Ripley.

00:18:44.386 --> 00:18:49.040
The Vanishing is an effective thriller because of&nbsp;
the toll it inflicts on a harmless protagonist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:54.480
The story follows a couple, Saskia and Rex, who&nbsp;
are on a trip together. When the two of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:59.920
pull over at a rest stop, Saskia goes into&nbsp;
the station to buy something and vanishes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:03.600
Rex frantically searches for her,&nbsp;
but is never able to find her.

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:08.960
Years later, Rex is confronted by Saskia's&nbsp;
kidnapper. Raymond, the man who kidnapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:13.920
and eventually killed Saskia, tells Rex that&nbsp;
he knew from a young age that he didn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:18.640
any conscience. In fact, his decision&nbsp;
to kidnap Saskia came as he was trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:24.000
to determine whether doing bad things felt&nbsp;
any different to him than doing good deeds.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:29.520
Eventually, he tells Rex that the only way he'll&nbsp;
be able to learn what happened to Saskia is by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:35.200
experiencing it himself. The two return to the&nbsp;
rest stop where Saskia originally vanished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:41.200
and Raymond drugs Rex. When he wakes up,&nbsp;
Rex finds that Raymond has buried him alive.

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:45.120
Raymond, meanwhile, is living a&nbsp;
quiet, peaceful life with his family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:48.640
He's gotten away with killing two&nbsp;
people, and he doesn't feel a thing.

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:55.600
Satan is usually a hard villain to&nbsp;
overcome. That much is obvious in The Omen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.600 --> 00:20:01.680
a classic horror film for many reasons, but one&nbsp;
is undoubtedly its bleak ending. The film tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:07.680
the story of a couple who unknowingly adopt&nbsp;
the antichrist, a young boy named Damien.

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:13.360
Robert Thorn, Damien's father, spends much of the&nbsp;
movie trying to determine where his adopted son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:19.920
actually came from. Along the way, a number of&nbsp;
people die under questionable circumstances.

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:23.600
"Look at me Damien. It's all for you."

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:29.680
As the movie approaches its climax, Damien's&nbsp;
adopted mother is killed by his nanny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:34.720
and when Robert discovers that his wife has&nbsp;
been killed, he stabs the nanny to death.

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:40.320
Then, he takes Damien to a church and places&nbsp;
him on the altar, only to be shot down by police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:45.760
before he can kill his demonic son. The movie&nbsp;
ends with a joint funeral for Damien's adopted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:52.080
family, and Damien himself cracking a smile. By&nbsp;
the end of The Omen, evil has conquered good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:56.560
and everyone who knows what Damien really&nbsp;
is has already ended up in the ground.

00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:03.200
When you watch Brazil, things are not exactly&nbsp;
what they seem. The movie follows a low-level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:07.280
government worker named Sam who spends&nbsp;
much of his time thinking about a woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:12.800
who comes to him in dreams. Ultimately, Sam&nbsp;
becomes embroiled in a complicated conspiracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:17.840
after discovering that a bureaucratic error has&nbsp;
led to the arrest and death of the wrong man.

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:22.480
That discovery leads Sam down a path to find&nbsp;
the woman he's been seeing in his dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:27.440
who is named Jill. When he learns that Jill&nbsp;
is considered a terrorist accomplice because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:33.120
she attempted to report the wrongful arrest,&nbsp;
Sam creates false records so she can escape.

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:36.880
After sharing a romantic night&nbsp;
together, the two are arrested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:41.920
and Sam is strapped to a chair where he is about&nbsp;
to be tortured. Before the torture can begin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:46.880
though, a resistance force breaks into&nbsp;
the building and rescues Sam and Jill.

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:48.640
In the movie's final moments, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:53.200
we learn that the rescue mission was&nbsp;
all a delusion of Sam's broken mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:58.400
We see that he is still trapped to the chair, and&nbsp;
it's heavily implied that he has been lobotomized.

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:03.680
Sam may dream of escaping the bureaucracy&nbsp;
that controls every aspect of his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:07.200
but in reality, it's a system&nbsp;
that's impossible to escape from.

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:14.560
Another horror entry, Creep's ending is dark&nbsp;
and definitive. This found-footage film tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:19.520
the story of Aaron, a videographer who has&nbsp;
been hired by a man named Josef to film a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:25.280
video diary in a remote cabin. Josef tells&nbsp;
Aaron that he has an inoperable brain tumor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:29.440
and wants to film a diary for his&nbsp;
daughter, who will be born after he dies.

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:34.560
Josef displays strange behavior throughout the&nbsp;
movie, and Aaron ultimately receives a call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:39.360
from Josef's sister urging him to leave the&nbsp;
house as soon as possible. The two scuffle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:44.720
but Aaron gets away, and begins getting&nbsp;
videotapes from Josef following his departure.

00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:50.640
Aaron realizes that Josef is stalking him, and&nbsp;
Josef eventually offers to meet Aaron in a public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:56.480
park in order to make peace. As a precaution,&nbsp;
Aaron decides to keep a camera on himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:00.880
Josef comes up from behind Aaron while he's&nbsp;
sitting on a bench and kills him with an axe.

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:06.160
Josef reviews the footage Aaron collected&nbsp;
following his death, and realizes that Aaron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.160 --> 00:23:11.360
must have thought he was a good person. The&nbsp;
movie ends as Josef moves on to his next kill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:14.560
and puts Aaron's film on the shelf&nbsp;
with the rest of his victims.

